Fix a use-after-free tearing down the libnice ICE transport.

libnice delivers received packets and emits its signals on the shared glib main loop, with the
IceTransport as user data. ~IceTransport() detaches the receive callback and removes the stream
from whatever thread destroys it, which does not synchronise with a callback the loop is already
dispatching: the in-flight receive then runs against a half-destroyed transport whose stream is
being removed underneath it. Observed as an intermittent SIGSEGV (~2-5% of runs) on the loop
thread:

  #0 __memmove_evex_unaligned_erms ()                          libc
  #1 rtc::make_message<std::byte*>(...)                        libdatachannel
  #2 rtc::impl::IceTransport::RecvCallback(_NiceAgent*, ...)   libdatachannel (libnice variant)
  #3 nice_component_emit_io_callback ()                        libnice
  #4 component_io_cb ()                                        libnice
  #6 g_main_context_dispatch_unlocked ()                       glib

Perform the teardown ON the loop thread and wait for it. A GMainContext dispatches from one
thread at a time, so while the detach runs the loop cannot be delivering anything, and once it
returns nothing can reach the object again. The signal handlers are disconnected there too, for
the same reason. g_main_context_invoke_full runs the function inline when the caller already owns
the context, so destroying from inside a libnice callback cannot deadlock.

Not upstreamed yet; libjuice (libdatachannel's default backend) does not have this race, so it
only affects USE_NICE=ON builds such as the one nixpkgs ships.

--- a/src/impl/icetransport.cpp
+++ b/src/impl/icetransport.cpp
@@ -13,9 +13,11 @@
 #include "utils.hpp"
 
 #include <algorithm>
+#include <future>
 #include <iostream>
 #include <random>
 #include <sstream>
+#include <utility>
 
 #ifdef _WIN32
 #include <winsock2.h>
@@ -655,13 +657,44 @@
 
 IceTransport::~IceTransport() {
 	PLOG_DEBUG << "Destroying ICE transport";
-	nice_agent_attach_recv(mNiceAgent.get(), mStreamId, 1, g_main_loop_get_context(MainLoop->get()),
-	                       NULL, NULL);
-	nice_agent_remove_stream(mNiceAgent.get(), mStreamId);
-	mNiceAgent.reset();
 
-	if (mTimeoutId)
-		g_source_remove(mTimeoutId);
+	// libnice delivers receives and emits its signals on the shared glib main loop, with `this`
+	// as user data. Detaching them from the destroying thread does not synchronise with a
+	// callback the loop is ALREADY dispatching, so an in-flight receive can run against a
+	// half-destroyed transport and a stream being removed underneath it — a use-after-free that
+	// crashes in RecvCallback, under nice_component_emit_io_callback.
+	//
+	// Perform the detach ON the loop thread instead, and wait for it. A GMainContext dispatches
+	// from one thread at a time, so while this runs the loop cannot be delivering anything, and
+	// once it returns nothing can reach this object again. g_main_context_invoke_full runs the
+	// function inline when the caller already owns the context, so destroying from within a
+	// libnice callback stays deadlock-free.
+	using Job = std::pair<IceTransport *, std::promise<void> *>;
+	std::promise<void> detached;
+	auto future = detached.get_future();
+	Job job(this, &detached);
+
+	g_main_context_invoke_full(
+	    g_main_loop_get_context(MainLoop->get()), G_PRIORITY_HIGH,
+	    [](gpointer data) -> gboolean {
+		    auto *job = static_cast<Job *>(data);
+		    IceTransport *transport = job->first;
+		    NiceAgent *agent = transport->mNiceAgent.get();
+		    g_signal_handlers_disconnect_by_data(G_OBJECT(agent), transport);
+		    nice_agent_attach_recv(agent, transport->mStreamId, 1,
+		                           g_main_loop_get_context(MainLoop->get()), NULL, NULL);
+		    nice_agent_remove_stream(agent, transport->mStreamId);
+		    if (transport->mTimeoutId) {
+			    g_source_remove(transport->mTimeoutId);
+			    transport->mTimeoutId = 0;
+		    }
+		    job->second->set_value();
+		    return G_SOURCE_REMOVE;
+	    },
+	    &job, nullptr);
+
+	future.wait();
+	mNiceAgent.reset();
 }
 
 Description::Role IceTransport::role() const { return mRole; }
